A) User Manual Procedure
Setting Up a GitHub Repository and Making a First Commit
Purpose
This procedure explains how to create a GitHub repository, connect it to a local project folder, and make the first commit.
Prerequisites
Before starting, you need:
A computer.
An internet connection.
A GitHub account.
Git installed on your computer.
A text editor such as Visual Studio Code or Notepad.
A project folder containing at least one file.
Basic knowledge of using a computer and opening a terminal or command prompt.
Step-by-Step Procedure
Step 1: Open GitHub
Action: Open GitHub in a web browser.
Expected result: The GitHub website opens successfully.
Step 2: Sign in to GitHub
Action: Sign in using your GitHub username and password.
Expected result: Your GitHub account homepage appears.
Step 3: Create a new repository
Action: Click the New repository option.
Expected result: The repository creation page appears.
Step 4: Enter the repository name
Action: Enter my-first-project as the repository name.
Expected result: The repository name appears in the repository name field.
Step 5: Select repository visibility
Action: Select Public as the repository visibility.
Expected result: The repository is configured as public.
Step 6: Create the repository
Action: Click Create repository.
Expected result: GitHub creates the repository and displays its repository page.
Step 7: Open the project folder
Action: Open the folder containing your project files.
Expected result: The project files are displayed.
Step 8: Open the terminal
Action: Open a terminal inside the project folder.
Expected result: The terminal opens in the project folder.
Step 9: Initialize Git
Action: Run git init.
Expected result: Git initializes the project as a local Git repository.
Step 10: Check the repository status
Action: Run git status.
Expected result: Git displays the files that are not currently tracked.
Step 11: Add the project files
Action: Run git add ..
Expected result: The project files are added to the staging area.
Step 12: Create the first commit
Action: Run git commit -m "Initial commit".
Expected result: Git creates the first commit.
Step 13: Copy the repository URL
Action: Copy the HTTPS URL from the GitHub repository page.
Expected result: The repository URL is copied.
Step 14: Connect the local repository to GitHub
Action: Run git remote add origin YOUR_REPOSITORY_URL.
Expected result: The local repository is connected to the GitHub repository.
Step 15: Rename the branch
Action: Run git branch -M main.
Expected result: The current branch is renamed to main.
Step 16: Push the commit
Action: Run git push -u origin main.
Expected result: The project files are uploaded to GitHub.
Step 17: Refresh the GitHub repository
Action: Refresh the repository page.
Expected result: The project files and the Initial commit message appear on GitHub.
Screenshot Description
A screenshot should show the GitHub repository page after the first push.
The screenshot should show:
The repository name my-first-project.
The uploaded project files.
The main branch.
The Initial commit message.
Troubleshooting
The most common beginner error is:
'git' is not recognized as an internal or external command
This usually means Git is not installed or is not correctly added to the computer's PATH.
To solve this problem, install Git, restart the terminal, and run:
git --version
If Git is installed correctly, the command displays the installed Git version.










B) API Reference Entry
Create a New Task
HTTP Method
POST
Endpoint
/api/v1/projects/{projectId}/tasks
Description
This endpoint allows an authenticated user to create a new task inside a project management application.
A task must have a title, an assignee, a due date, and a priority. The description is optional.
The system automatically generates the task ID, status, creation time, and update time.
Path Parameters
Parameter
Data Type
Required
Description
projectId
string
Yes
Unique ID of the project where the task will be created.

Query Parameters
This endpoint does not require any query parameters.
Request Headers
Header
Required
Description
Authorization
Yes
Contains the authentication token using the Bearer scheme.
Content-Type
Yes
Specifies that the request body is JSON.
Accept
Yes
Specifies that the client expects a JSON response.

Request Body Parameters
Parameter
Data Type
Required
Description
title
string
Yes
The name or title of the task.
description
string
No
Additional information about the task.
assigneeId
string
Yes
ID of the user assigned to the task.
dueDate
string
Yes
The deadline for completing the task in YYYY-MM-DD format.
priority
string
Yes
Task priority. Allowed values are low, medium, or high.

Example Request
POST /api/v1/projects/proj_1024/tasks
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
{
  "title": "Complete database documentation",
  "description": "Write and review the documentation for the project database.",
  "assigneeId": "usr_2048",
  "dueDate": "2026-10-15",
  "priority": "high"
}
HTTP Response Codes
201 Created
The task was successfully created.
400 Bad Request
The request contains invalid or missing data.
401 Unauthorized
The authentication credentials are missing or invalid.
403 Forbidden
The authenticated user does not have permission to create tasks in the project.
404 Not Found
The specified project or assignee does not exist.
409 Conflict
The request conflicts with the current state of the project.
422 Unprocessable Entity
The request contains valid JSON but the supplied data fails validation.
500 Internal Server Error
An unexpected error occurred on the server.
